== Marital Unity and Sexual Faithfulness

=== §. 1. #emph[Of the heads of those common-mutuall duties.]

IN the first part of this Treatise concerning #emph[Man] and #emph[Wife,] hath beene declared, who are so to be accounted: in this second part their #emph[common-mutuall duties] are to be laid forth. These are either #footnote[De necessitate ad esse.] #emph[absolutely necessary] for the #emph[being] and #emph[abiding] of mariage: or #footnote[De honestate ad benè esse.] needfull and requisite for the #emph[well being] and #emph[well abiding] of it, that is, for the good estate of mariage, and for a commendable, and comfortable liuing together.

There are two kindes of the former

+ #emph[Matrimoniall Vnity.]
+ #emph[Matrimoniall Chastity.]

The latter also may be drawne to two heads: for they are either such as the maried couple are #emph[mutually] to performe #emph[each to other:] or such as both of them are #emph[ioyntly] to performe to #emph[others.]

Those mutuall duties are

+ #emph[A louing affection of one to another.]
+ #emph[A prouident care of one for another.]

Vnder that #emph[prouident care] I comprise both the #emph[Meanes] whereby it may be the better effected (which is #emph[Cohabitation)] and the #emph[Matter] wherein it consisteth:

And this respecteth

+ The #emph[Soule]
+ The #emph[Body]

+ The #emph[Good-name]
+ The #emph[Goods]

of each other.

The #emph[ioynt] duties which are to be performed #emph[to others,]

respect

+ Those who are #emph[in the house.]
+ Those who are #emph[out of the house.]

They who are in the house are

+ #emph[Members] of the familie.
+ #emph[Guests] which come to the familie.

Many more particulars are comprised vnder these generall heads, which I purpose distinctly to deliuer, as I come to them in their seuerall proper places.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of Matrimoniall vnitie.]

The first, highest, chiefest, and most absolutely necessarie common-mutuall dutie betwixt Man and Wife, is #emph[Matrimoniall Vnitie,] whereby husband and wife doe account one another to be #emph[one flesh,] and accordingly preserue the inuiolable vnion whereby they are knit together. This is that dutie which the Apostle inioyneth to husbands and wiues, in these words, #emph[Let] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 10, 11.] #emph[not the wife depart from her husband: Let not the husband put away his wife.] He there speaketh of renouncing each other, and making the matrimoniall bond frustrate, and of no effect: which bond he would haue to be kept firme and inuiolable, and they #emph[two] who are thereby made #emph[one,] constantly to remaine #emph[one,] and not to make themselues #emph[two] againe. This #emph[matrimoniall vnitie] is so necessarie, as it may not be dis-united or dissolued though one be a Christian, the other a Pagan. #emph[If any brother] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 12, 13.] (saith the Apostle) #emph[hath a wife that beleeueth not, let him not put her away. And the woman which hath an husband that beleeueth not, let her not leaue him.]

The reasons of this inuiolable vnion are especially two: One taken from the #emph[Author] of mariage: the other from the #emph[Nature] thereof.

1. The #emph[Author] of mariage is God. It is his ordinance: and #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 18, 22, 24] he it is that by his ordinance hath made of #emph[two, one flesh.] Now marke the consequence which Christ as a ruled case, and vndeniable principle inferreth thereon, #emph[What God hath ioyned together] #footnote[#emph[Mat] 19. 6.] #emph[let no man put asunder:] If #emph[no man,] then nor wife, nor husband himselfe.

2. Such is the #emph[Nature] of the matrimoniall bond as it maketh of #emph[two one,] and #footnote[See Treat. 1 §. 79, 80.] more firmely bindeth them two together, then any other bond can binde any other two together, how then should they be #emph[two] againe?

=== §. 3. #emph[Of Desertion.]

The vice contrary to matrimoniall vnitie is #emph[Desertion,] when one of the maried couple through indignation of the true religion, and vtter detestation thereof, or some other like cause, shall apparently renounce all matrimoniall vnitie, and withdraw him or her selfe from all societie with the other, and liue among Infidels, Idolaters, heretiques, or other such persecutors, as a faithfull Christian with safetie of life, or a good conscience, cannot abide among; and though all good meanes that can be thought of be vsed to reclaime the partie so departed, yet nothing will preuaile, but obstinatly persisteth in renouncing all matrimoniall fellowship.

This #emph[Desertion] is in the case of mariage so capitall, as it #footnote[#emph[Infidelis discedens in matrimonium peccare dignoscitur: itaque non est ei fides seruanda coniugij. Ambr. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] freeth the innocent partie from any further seeking after the other. In which respect the Apostle saith, #emph[If the vnbeleeuing depart, let him depart. A brother or a sister is not vnder bondage in such cases,] 1 Cor. 7. 15. By bondage he meaneth matrimoniall subiection (by reason whereof neither of the maried persons haue power of their owne body, but one of the others.) Now they that are not vnder this bondage, are not bound to seeke after it. That #emph[Desertion] therefore on the delinquents #footnote[#emph[Phot. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[Luth. de matrim. Calu. epist.] 227. #emph[Beza in lib. de Diuort.]] part is such dissolution of mariage, as freeth the innocent partie from the bondage thereof. In many reformed Churches beyond the seas #emph[Desertion] is accounted so farre to dissolue the very bond of mariage, as libertie is giuen to the partie forsaken to marie another: and it is also applied to other cases then that which is aboue mentioned: as when an #footnote[#emph[P. Martyr in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 13. #emph[Melanct. in loc. de Coniug.]] Infidell, Idolater, or Heretique shall depart from one of the true religion for other causes then hatred of religion: or when both man and wife hauing liued as Idolaters among Idolaters, #footnote[#emph[Zanchius de Oper. Dei lib.] 4. #emph[cap.] 1.] one of them being conuerted to the true faith, leaueth his abode among Idolaters, and goeth to the professors of the true faith, but can by no meanes get the other partie to remoue: or when one of the true religion shall depart from another of the same profession, and will by no meanes be brought to liue with the partie so left, but openly manifesteth peremptorie obstinacie; the matter being heard and adiudged by the Magistrate, the mariage bond may be broken, and libertie giuen to the partie forsaken to mary another. But because our Church hath no such custome, nor our law determined such cases, I leaue them to the custome of other Churches.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of matrimoniall Chastitie.]

The second necessarie common-mutuall mariage-dutie is #emph[Matrimoniall chastitie. Chastitie] in a large extent is taken for all manner of puritie in soule or body: in which respect the Apostle calleth the Church of God #emph[a chaste virgin.] But in the #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 11. 2.] sense wherein we here vse it, it especially appertaineth to the body: which is that vertue whereby #emph[we possesse our vessels] (to #footnote[1 #emph[Thess.] 4. 4.] vse the Apostles phrase) #emph[in holinesse and honour:] or more plainly to our purpose, whereby we keepe our bodies vndefiled.

Chastitie thus restrained to the body is of

+ #emph[Single life.]
+ #emph[Wedlocke.]

That of single life is opposed to fornication: and it is either of such as neuer were maried. Such an one was #emph[S. Paul,] in which respect he wisheth that all were as he, 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 7. Or of such as are lawfully freed from the bond of mariage. Such an one the Apostle calleth #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 5. #emph[Paphnutius congressum cum legitima vxore castitatem esse dicebat. Socr. hist. Eccl. l.] 1. #emph[cap.] 11.] #emph[a widow indeed.] Chastitie of wedlocke is that vertue whereby parties maried, obseruing the lawfull and honest vse of mariage, keepe their bodies from being defiled with strange flesh: thus the Apostle commandeth #emph[wiues] to be #emph[chaste, Tit.] 2. 5. So as #footnote[Qui leges coniugij seruat homini continenti par sit. Greg. Naz. Cygn. Carm.] they that keepe the lawes of wedlocke are as chaste as they that containe.

Here by the way note the dotage of our aduersaries, who thinke there is no chastitie, but of single persons: whereupon in their speeches and writings #footnote[#emph[Clerici castitatem professi matrimonium contrahere non possunt. Concil. Trid.] § 24. #emph[Can] 9.] they oppose chastitie and matrimonie one to another, as two contraries.

Some of their holy Fathers and Popes, and those not the least learned, nor of worst note among them, haue inferred by their arguments against Priests mariage, that #emph[Mariage is a liuing in the flesh, a sowing to the flesh, a pollution of the flesh.] To that #footnote[#emph[Siricius & Innocentius] 3. #emph[Gratian. Decr. distinct.] 31. 82. 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 5.] purpose #emph[S. Pauls] aduice to man and wife to abstaine, that they may giue themselues to fasting and prayer, is vrged: but directly contrarie to the intent of the Apostle. For,

1. He speaketh there of extraordinarie humiliation. 2. He interposeth this limitation, #emph[for a time.] 3. He saith not simply, #emph[that ye may pray,] but #emph[that ye may giue your selues.] (or haue #footnote[#emph[Non dixit simpliciter vt oretis, sed vt vacetis orationi: ac sirem impediat, non coinquinet. Chrys in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] leasure) #emph[to prayer:] as if it did only hinder, but not pollute prayer.

But how can the forenamed spots and blots of mariage stand with that beautie and glorie wherewith the Apostle setteth it forth in these words, #emph[Mariage is honourable in all, Heb.] 13. 4. If mariage were as Papists set it forth to be, the #emph[mariagebed] were verie vnfitly called #emph[a bed vndefiled.]

Behold how contrarie the spirits of #emph[S. Paul] and of their Popes were. I wot well farre more contrarie then chastitie and matrimonie.

But to returne to our matter, cleare it is, that maried persons may be chaste, and accordingly they ought to be chaste. To which purpose the Apostle counselling men and women, for auoiding fornication, to haue wiues and husbands, inserteth this particle OWNE #emph[(Let euerie man haue his] OWNE #emph[wife,] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2..] #emph[and euerie woman haue her] OWNE #emph[husband)] whereby he implieth, that they should not haue to doe with any other. That which #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 5. 19. See more of this text in Treat. 4. §. 11.] #emph[Salomon] expresseth of an husband, by the rule of relation must be applied to a wife. As the man must be satisfied at all times in his wife, and euen rauisht with her loue; so must the woman be satisfied at all times in her husband, and euen rauisht with his loue. By the like rule the precept giuen to wiues, #emph[to be chaste,] must husbands take as directed to themselues #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 2. 5.] also, and be #emph[chaste.] This dutie did #emph[Isaak] and #emph[Rebekah] faithfully and mutually performe each to other.

1. It was one maine end, why mariage (especially since the #footnote[Reasons for mariage-chastitie.] fall of man) was ordained, to liue #emph[chastely.] This the Apostle implieth, where he saith, #emph[to auoid fornication,] let euerie man haue his owne wife, and let euerie woman haue her owne husband. #footnote[1\. Chastitie one end of mariage. 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2. #emph[Ibid. vers.] 9.] And againe, #emph[If they cannot containe let them marrie.]

2. By chastitie is a godly seed preserued on earth. By this reason doth the Prophet #emph[Malachie] inforce this dutie. For after he had said, that the Lord #emph[made one,] meaning of two one flesh #footnote[2\. By chastitie a godly seed preserued. #emph[Mal.] 2. 16.] by mariage, he inferreth this exhortation, #emph[Therefore take heed to your spirit, and let none deale vnfaithfully against the wife of his youth.]

3. An especiall part of the honour of mariage consisteth in #footnote[3\. Chastitie the honour of mariage. #emph[Heb.] 13. 4.] chastitie: whereupon the Apostle hauing giuen this high commendation of mariage, that it is #emph[honourable in all,] addeth this clause #emph[(and the bed vndefiled)] to shew the reason of that honour. As if he had said, Because the mariage-bed is in it selfe a bed vndefiled, mariage is therefore in it selfe honourable, and doth so farre remaine honourable, as the bed remaineth vndefiled.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of Adulterie.]

The vice contrarie to matrimoniall chastitie is #emph[Adulterie,] one of the most capitall vices in that estate: a vice whereby way is made for #emph[Diuorce:] as is cleare and euident by the determination of Christ himselfe, concerning that point, first #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 5. 32.] propounded in his sermon on the mount, and againe #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 19. 9. #emph[Christus ab adultera vero separari permittit. Greg. Naz. Orat.] 36. #emph[Vir post fornicationem non est vir, Chrys. hom.] 19. #emph[in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] repeated in his conference with the Pharisies, where condemning vniust diuorces, he excepteth the diuorce made for adulterie.

And great reason there is thereof. For #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] the adulterer maketh himselfe one flesh with his harlot. Why then should he remaine to be one flesh with his wife? #emph[Two] (saith the Law) #emph[shall be one flesh:] not three. The like may be said of a wife committing adulterie.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of pardoning adulterie vpon repentance.]

#emph[Quest.] Seeing by adulterie iust cause of diuorce is giuen, may this fault vpon the repentance of the delinquent person be so forgiuen, as no diuorce be sought by the innocent person, but both continue to liue together in wedlocke as before?

#emph[Answ.] Though it be not meet in this case, to impose it as an inuiolable law vpon the innocent partie, to retaine the delinquent, because of repentance (for we haue direct and strict warrant for it) yet I doubt not but they may so doe, if they will, and that without iust exception to the contrarie, they ought so to doe. For the law of diuorce did not necessarily enioyne any to sue out the bill, but only afforded them libertie to vse that punishment if they saw cause. I doubt not but for warrant of this libertie, we may take Gods patterne, in retaining Churches and people after they haue committed spirituall adulterie: and Christs forgiuing the woman that had committed adulterie. For, #footnote[#emph[Postquam Christus ait adulterae, nec ego te damnabo: vade, deinceps noli peccare, quis non intelligat, debere maritum ignoscere quod videt ignouisse Dominum ambor um: nec iam se debere adulteram dicere cuius poenitentis crimen diuina credit miseratione deletam? Aug. de adult. Coniug. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 6.] #emph[Seeing Christ said to an adult eresse, I condemne thee not, goe and sinne no more, who cannot conceiue that an husband ought to] #emph[forgiue that which he seeth the Lord both of husband and wife hath forgiuen: and that he ought not to account her an adulteresse, whose fault he beleeueth to be blotted out, by the mercie of God, vpon her repentance?]

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the difference of adultery in a man, and in a wife.]

#emph[Quest.] Is the bond of mariage as much violated on the #footnote[Adultery as great a sinne in a man as in his wife. #emph[Lex Jul. de Adult. l.] 1. #emph[Orif. Codic. in l. Illust. Greg. Sayr. Clau. reg. l.] 9. #emph[c.] 16.] mans part when he committeth adulterie as on the womans when she doth so?

#emph[Answ.] Though the ancient Romans and Canonists haue aggrauated the womans fault in this kinde farre aboue the mans, and giuen the man more priuiledges then the woman, yet I see not how that difference in the sinne can stand with the tenour of Gods word. I denie not but that more inconueniences may follow vpon the womans default then vpon the mans: as, greater infamy before men, worse disturbance #footnote[More inconueniences of a womans adultery, then a mans.] of the family, more mistaking of legitimate, or illegitimate children, with the like. The man cannot so well know which be his owne children, as the woman; he may take base children to be his owne, and so cast the inheritance vpon them; and suspect his owne to be basely borne, and so depriue them of their patrimony. But the woman is freed from all such mistakings. Yet in regard of the breach of wedlocke, and transgression against God, the sinne of either partie is alike. Gods word maketh no disparity betwixt them. At the beginning God said of them both, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 5.] #emph[they two shall be one flesh:] not the woman only with the man, but the man also with the woman is made #emph[one flesh.] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 3, 4.] Their power also ouer one another in this respect is alike. If on iust occasion they abstaine, #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 5.] it must be with mutuall consent. If the husband leaue his wife, #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 15.] she is as free, as he should be, if she left him. Accordingly the punishment #footnote[#emph[Tanto grauius viros puniri oportet, quanto magis ad eos pertinet & virtute vincere, & exemplo regere soeminas. Aug. de adult. Coning. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 8.] which by Gods law was to be inflicted on Adulterers is the same, whether the man or the woman be the delinquent, #emph[(Deut.] 22. 22.) If difference be made, it is meet that adulterous husbands be so much the more seuerely punished, by how much the more it appertaineth to them to excell in vertue, and to gouerne their wiues by example.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the hainousnesse of Adulterie.]

But to returne to the discouery of the hainousnesse of Adulterie, I finde no sinne thorowout the whole Scripture so notoriously in the seuerall colours thereof set forth, as it is. For besides that it is #footnote[7\. Commandement.] by name forbidden in the Decalogue, it is further expresly branded to be committed,

1. Against #emph[each person in the holy Trinitie:] the #emph[Father] (whose #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 2. 17.] couenant is broken) the #emph[Sonne] (whose #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] members are made the members of an harlot) and the #emph[Holy Ghost] (whose #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 19.] Temple is polluted.)

2. Against ones #emph[neighbour,] as #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 34. 31.] the partie with whom the sinne is committed (for this sinne cannot be committed singly by one alone) the #emph[husband] and #emph[wife] of each partie #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 6. 35.] who cannot rest contented with any satisfaction) the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 21. 10. #emph[Iudg.] 11. 2. #emph[Deut.] 23. 2.] #emph[children] borne in adulterie (whom they brand with an indelible character of infamie, and depriue of many priuiledges that otherwise they might enioy) the #footnote[#emph[Leuit.] 21. 9. #emph[Gen.] 34. 7. 2 #emph[Sam.] 13. 22.] #emph[alliance] and #emph[friends] of each partie (to whom the griefe and disgrace of this foule sinne reacheth) the #emph[whole family] appertaining to either of them (for #footnote[#emph[Iob] 31. 12. 2 #emph[Sam.] 12. 10.] this is as a fire in an house) the #emph[towne, citie,] and #emph[nation] where such vncleane birds roost (for #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 34. 27. #emph[Iudg.] 20. 10. #emph[Hos.] 4. 3.] all they lie open to the vengeance of God for this sinne) and the very #emph[Church of God] (the #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 15.] holy seed whereof is by this sinne hindred)

3. Against the #emph[parties] themselues that commit this sinne, and that against their #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 6. 32.] soules, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 18.] bodies, #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 6. 33.] name, #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 6. 26.] goods, and all that appertaineth to them.

As this sinne is in it selfe a sinfull sinne, so by the bitter and #footnote[The bitter fruits of Adulterie.] cursed fruits which proceed from it, it is made out of measure sinfull. For

1. By it husbands and wiues affection (which of all other ought to be the most inuiolable) is so alienated, as seldome it is reconciled againe.

2. By it the goods of the family are much wasted: the adulterous husband spending that wherewith he should prouide for his family, on his harlot: and the adulterous wife purloining what she can from her husband.

3. By it husbands and wiues are stirred vp to wish, and long after one anothers death: and not only inwardly in heart to wish it, but outwardly also in deed to practise it.

4. If from this sinne there arise not a gauling and terrifying conscience (as oft there doth) then (which is worse) a seared conscience, an hard heart, a reprobate sense, and an impudent face.

Wherefore God accordingly deales with such sinners. In #footnote[Gods iudgements against adulterie.] his soule #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 22. 14.] he #emph[hateth them:] by his word he hath denounced many fearefull iudgements against them, both in #footnote[#emph[Hos.] 4. 2, 3.] this world, and in #footnote[#emph[Reu.] 21. 8.] the world to come (against no sinne more.) This sin is reckoned to be one of the most principall causes of the greatest iudgements that euer were inflicted in the world: as of the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 6. 2, #emph[&c.]] generall deluge: of that #footnote[#emph[Ezek.] 16. 50.] fire and brimstone which destroied #emph[Sodome] and #emph[Gomorrah:] of #footnote[#emph[Leuit.] 18. 28.] Canaans spuing out her Inhabitants: of that #footnote[#emph[Num.] 25. 9.] plague which destroied in one day 24000: and of the #footnote[#emph[Ier.] 5. 7, 8, 9.] Israelites captiuitie, with the like: By all which we see that fearefull doome verified, #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 13. 4.] #emph[Whoremongers, and adulterers God will iudge.] Now consider what #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 10. 31.] #emph[a fearefull thing it is to fall into the hands of the liuing God.]

=== §. 9. #emph[Of remedies against Adulterie, and in particular of] due beneuolence, #emph[and of defect or excesse therein.]

For preuenting this hainous sinne (to omit many other remedies which Gods word hath prescribed, as #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 6. 25.] a diligent keeping of the #emph[heart] (that lustfull thoughts proceed not from thence) of the #footnote[#emph[Iob] 31. 1.] #emph[eies] (that they wander not on the beautie or propernesse of any ones person, or on lasciuious pictures, or any other like allurements) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 21.] #emph[eares] (that they hearken not to any inticements of others) of the #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 5. 4.] #emph[tongue] (that it vtter no vnchaste and corrupt communication) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 13.] #emph[lips] (that they delight not in wanton kisses) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 6. 29.] #emph[hands] (that they vse no wanton daliance) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 25.] #emph[feet] (that they carry thee not too neere to the place where adulterie may be committed) of thy #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 5. 7.] #emph[company] (that thou be not defiled with others wantonnesse and vncleannesse) of thy #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 19. 33.] #emph[diet] (that it be not immoderate) of thine #footnote[#emph[Ezek.] 23. 6, 15, 40.] #emph[apparell] (that it be not garish and lasciuious) of thy #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 39. 9. #emph[Pro.] 2. 16.] #emph[time] (that it be not vainly and idly spent) to omit, I say, these and other like remedies.) One of the best remedies that can be prescribed to maried persons (next to an #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 39. 9. #emph[Pro.] 2. 16.] awfull feare of God, and a continuall setting of him before them, wheresoeuer they are) is, that husband and wife mutually delight each in other, and maintaine a pure and feruent loue betwixt themselues, yeelding that #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2, 3, 5, 9.] #emph[due beneuolence] one to another which is warranted & sanctified by Gods word, and ordained of God for this particular end. This #emph[due beneuolence] (as the Apostle stileth it) is one of the most proper and essentiall acts of mariage: and necessary for the maine and principall ends thereof: as for preseruation of chastitie in such as haue not the gift of continency, for increasing the world with a legitimate brood, and for linking the affections of the maried couple more firmely together. These ends of mariage, at least the two former, are made void without this duty be performed.

As it is called #emph[beneuolence] because it must be performed with good will and delight, willingly, readily and cheerefully; so it is said to be #emph[due] because it is a debt which the wife oweth to her husband, and he to her. #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 4.] #emph[For the wife hath not the power of her owne body, but the husband; and likewise also the husband hath not the power of his owne body, but the wife.]

I haue my warrant from the Apostle to prescribe this dutie as a remedie against adulterie. For #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2, 3.] #emph[to auoid fornication,] he aduiseth man and wife to #emph[render due beneuolence] one to another. If then this question be moued #emph[(How will mariage keep men and women from adulterie?)] this answer out of the Apostles words may be giuen #emph[(by rendring due beneuolence:)] which he further inculcateth by declaring the mischiefe that may follow vpon the neglect of this dutie, namely #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 5.] #emph[a casting of themselues into the snares of Satan.] Well might he presse this dutie to that end, because no other meanes is of like force: nor fasting, nor watching, nor hard lodging, nor long trauell, nor much labour, nor cold, nor solitarinesse, nor any thing else. #footnote[Dum solitudinis me deserta vallarent, ardorem naturae ferre non poteram: quem cùm crebris ieiunijs frangerem, mens cogitationibus aestuabat. Hier. ad Rust. Mon. Lege eundem ad Eustoc. de custod. virgin.] Some that haue by these meanes endeuoured much to beat downe their bodies, and subdue lust (but neglected the forenamed remedie) haue notwithstanding felt lust boiling in them.

There are two extremes contrarie to this dutie. One in the #emph[defect:] another in the #emph[excesse.]

#emph[Defect] therein is, when in case of need it is not required, or being required by the one, it is not yeelded by the other. Modestie is pretended by some for not requiring it: but in a duty so warrantable and needfull, pretence of modesty is (to speake the least) a signe of great infirmitie, and a cause of much iniquitie. To denie this dutie being iustly required, is to denie a due debt, and to giue Satan great aduantage. The punishment inflicted on #emph[Onan, (Gen.] 38. 9, 10.) sheweth how great a wrong this is. From that punishment #footnote[#emph[Hebraei inquiunt perinde vt homicidam reum esse qui temerè semen profundit. Merc. in Gen.] 38. 9.] the Hebrews gather that this sinne is a kinde of murther. It is so much the more hainous when hatred, stoutnesse, nicenesse, feare of hauing too many children, or any other like respects, are the cause thereof.

#emph[Excesse] is either in the #emph[measure,] or in the #emph[time. In the measure,] when husband or wife is insatiable; prouoking, rather then asswaging lust, and weakning their naturall vigor more then suppressing their vnnaturall humor. Many husbands and wiues are much oppressed by their bedfellowes vnsatiablenesse in this kinde.

#emph[In the time,] when it is against #emph[Pietie, Mercy,] or #emph[Modestie.]

1. #emph[Against Pietie,] when no day, nor dutie of Religion, no not extraordinarie daies, and duties of humiliation, will make them forbeare. #footnote[#emph[Ioel] 2. 6.] The Prophets bidding #emph[the bridegroome and bride goe out of their chamber in the day of a Fast,] and #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 5.] the Apostles #emph[excepting of Praier and fasting,] where he enioineth this dutie of due beneuolence, shew that in the time of a Fast it must be forborne.

2. #emph[Against Mercy,] when one of the married couple being weake by sicknesse, paine, labour, trauell, or any other like meanes, and through that weaknesse not well able to performe his dutie, the other notwithstanding will haue it performed. #emph[I will haue mercy, and not sacrifice,] saith the Lord. Shall Gods sacrifice giue place to mercy, and shall not mans or womans sacrifice? for so I may well terme this vnseasonable desire.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if an husband or wife continue so long sicke, or otherwise weake, as the other cannot containe?]

#emph[Answ.] In such cases of necessitie the body must be beaten owne, and earnest praier made for the gift of continency: for assuredly the Lord who hath brought thee to that necessitie, all giue thee grace sufficient.

3. #emph[Against Modesty,] #footnote[#emph[Quo tempore si vir coierit cun muliere, dicuntur concepti foetus vitium seminis tral ere: ita vt leprosi & elephantiaci ex hac conceptione nascantur. Hier. in Lam.] 1.] when husbands require this duty in that me, which vnder the Law was called #emph[the time of a wiues separation for her disease (Leu.] 15. 19. &c.) For what can be expected from such polluted copulation, but a leprous and loathsome generation? This kinde of intemperancie is expresly forbidden #emph[(Leuit.] 18. 19.) and a capitall punishment inflicted on such as offended therein #emph[(Leuit.] 20. 18.) Abstinence in this time is set in the catalogue of those notes which declare a man to be righteous #emph[(Eccles.] 20. 7.) and the contrary intemperancy is put in the roll of such abominations as prouoked God to spue out the Canaanits #emph[(Leuit.] 18. 28.) and to forsake his owne inheritance #emph[(Ezek.] 22. 10.)

To this kinde of intemperancie #footnote[#emph[Aug. de bon. coniug. ca.] 6. #emph[Amb. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] some referre a mans knowing of his wife after she hath conceiued with childe. But I finde no such matter condemned in Gods word: neither dare I make that a sinne which is not there condemned. #footnote[#emph[Nemo Essenorum cun praegnante concumbit. Ioseph. de bel. Iud. li.] 2. #emph[c.] 7.] Certaine Sectaries among the Iewes are branded for this error.

#strong[1. #emph[Obiect.] No other creature will so doe: so as it may seeme to be against nature.]

#emph[Answ.] 1. I denie the argument: though some forbeare, yet all doe not.

2. I denie the consequence: for other creatures are not so tied one male to one female, as an husband to his owne wife. #footnote[#emph[Quod in bestijs ipso naturae iure praescriptun est, vt praegnantes ad partum vsque non coeant, hoc in hominibus sciant arbitrio relictum. Hier. in Eph.] 5. #emph[Sanchez de Matr. lib.] 9. #emph[disp.] 21, 22. #emph[agit de petendo & reddendo debito quando menstruum est: & tempore praegnationis, & dun infans lactatur.]] Besides, that which beasts by nature are tied vnto, must be left to mans discretion.

#strong[2. #emph[Obiect.] After a woman hath once conceiued, no more conceptions can be expected, till she be deliuered.]

#emph[Answ.] Conception is not the only end of this dutie: for it is to be rendred to such as are barren.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if the wife giue sucke to her childe, ought not her husband then to forbeare?]

#emph[Answ.] Because giuing sucke is a mother dutie, man ought to doe what he can to containe.
